Nx formats code when you run `nx format:write` or `nx format:check`, at the end of most generators,
over the files `nx init` writes, and once over a workspace `create-nx-workspace` has just created.
All of them use the same formatter, and Nx works out which one that is by looking at your
workspace.

Two formatters are supported: [oxfmt](https://oxc.rs) and [Prettier](https://prettier.io).
New workspaces created with `create-nx-workspace` default to oxfmt.

## How Nx picks a formatter

Detection runs in this order, and the first match wins:

1. An oxfmt config file exists at the workspace root.
2. A Prettier config file exists at the workspace root, or `package.json` has a `prettier` key.
3. `package.json` declares an `oxfmt` dependency.

If nothing matches, `nx format:write` and `nx format:check` print a warning and exit `0`, and
generators skip formatting.
A workspace using Biome, dprint, or any other formatter lands here, which is what keeps Nx from
reformatting files it doesn't own.

The oxfmt config files Nx looks for:

- `.oxfmtrc.json`
- `.oxfmtrc.jsonc`
- `oxfmt.config.ts`
- `oxfmt.config.mts`

These are the names oxfmt itself searches for. It accepts other extensions when you point at one
explicitly with `-c`, but it never goes looking for them, so Nx doesn't either. Keep one config per
directory: oxfmt refuses to choose between two and fails with `Both '.oxfmtrc.json' and
'.oxfmtrc.jsonc' found`.

Step 3 has no Prettier equivalent, and that asymmetry is deliberate.
Prettier shows up in `node_modules` as a transitive dependency of all sorts of packages, so treating
its presence as intent would reformat workspaces that never asked for it.
oxfmt is rare enough as a transitive dependency that depending on it reads as a choice.

### When both are configured

oxfmt wins.
Adding an `.oxfmtrc.json` to a workspace that already has a `.prettierrc` switches the whole
workspace over, including generator formatting.
To go back, delete the oxfmt config.

## Switch an existing workspace to oxfmt

Install oxfmt, add a config file, and remove the Prettier one:

```shell
npm add -D oxfmt
```

```json {% title=".oxfmtrc.json" %}
{
  "singleQuote": true,
  "printWidth": 80
}
```

Those two values are what `@nx/js:init` writes, and they match the Prettier setup Nx generates.
The oxfmt defaults differ, so set them explicitly if you want your existing formatting to stay put.

Then delete `.prettierrc`, and run `nx format:write` to reformat everything in one commit.

Keep `.prettierignore` if you have one. oxfmt reads it too, so deleting it starts formatting files
you had excluded.

## Generator formatting and `nx format` differ

`nx format:write` runs the formatter's own CLI.
Generators format files that exist only in memory, so they call the oxfmt API directly and Nx applies
the surrounding configuration itself.
The two agree on the workspace root config, its `overrides` and `ignorePatterns`, the root
`.editorconfig`, and the root `.gitignore` and `.prettierignore`.

They differ in four cases. Three are about configuration that lives somewhere other than the
workspace root:

- An `.oxfmtrc.json` in a subdirectory applies under `nx format:write` but not during generator
  formatting.
- A `.gitignore` in a subdirectory applies under `nx format:write` but not during generator
  formatting.
- `nx format:write` uses the nearest `.editorconfig` found by walking up from the directory you run
  it in, so one above the workspace root applies when the workspace root has none of its own.
  Generator formatting reads only the workspace root file.

The fourth is at the root. An `.editorconfig` that sets `indent_size` without `indent_style` is
honoured by generator formatting, which follows the EditorConfig spec, and ignored by
`nx format:write`, which follows oxfmt. A workspace that relies on that shape gets generated files
its own `nx format:check` rejects. Set `indent_style` alongside it to keep the two in agreement.

Two things about `.editorconfig` may surprise you, and both come from oxfmt rather than from Nx: it
does not merge the chain, and it does not consult `root = true`. Any `.editorconfig` at the
workspace root stops the search, even an empty or comment-only one, and `root = true` in a parent
does not stop that parent from applying. oxfmt also resolves `.editorconfig` once from the working
directory rather than per formatted file, so a subdirectory's own file is ignored when you format it
from the workspace root. Running `nx format:write` from inside that subdirectory picks it up
instead. Generator formatting always reads the workspace root file.

## Skip formatting

Set [`NX_SKIP_FORMAT=true`](/docs/reference/environment-variables) to stop generators and migrations
from formatting.
It does not turn off TypeScript path sorting, which is a separate concern controlled by the
`sortRootTsconfigPaths` option and `NX_FORMAT_SORT_TSCONFIG_PATHS`, and is off by default.

```shell
NX_SKIP_FORMAT=true nx g @nx/react:app my-app
```

## Choose a formatter at workspace creation

`create-nx-workspace` asks which formatter you want when the workspace uses package manager
workspaces, which is the default.
With `--no-workspaces` it picks oxfmt without asking.
Pass `--formatter` to skip the prompt:

```shell
npx create-nx-workspace@latest my-workspace --formatter=prettier
```

The accepted values are `oxfmt`, `prettier`, and `none`.
